---

Running `ix apply` with no arguments in fifteen of this repo's example
directories did not converge anything. It failed on

```
flake '...' does not provide attribute 'packages.x86_64-linux.ix.default.config',
'legacyPackages.x86_64-linux.ix.default.config' or 'ix.default.config'
```

naming an attribute path nobody typed. `ix apply` picks between two paths by
looking for an `ix.default` binding in your flake: where it finds one it builds
`ix.default.config.system.build.toplevel` from it, and where it does not it
converges every `nixosConfigurations` entry that sets `ix.networking`. It reads
whether the binding exists, never what it is bound to — and `index.lib.mkVm`,
`mkDev` and `mkFleet` all return a fleet result, which has no `config`. Every
example that bound `ix.default` bound one of those.

The binding is gone from all fifteen, so a bare `ix apply` in any of them now
converges the nodes the flake declares. If you copied one of these flakes into
your own repo, delete the `ix.default = vm;` line and keep
`inherit (vm) nixosConfigurations;`. If you want the single-VM path instead,
bind the configuration rather than the fleet:
`ix.default = vm.nixosConfigurations.<name>;`, which is what `ix init`
scaffolds.

`tests/ix-default-is-a-vm.nix` now walks the repo for flakes, calls each one's
real `outputs`, and fails the eval naming any file whose `ix.default` has no
`config`. The CLI error itself is unchanged and still illegible; ENG-12208
carries that.
